   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "# LayerStream test 1 - big model on a small VRAM budget (Krea2, 24.5 GB bf16)\n\n**Weights** stream because of how ComfyUI is started, **activations** (they grow with image size) are handled by the `LayerStream: Krea2 Chunking` node + tiled VAE decode.\n\nOn a 24 GB card with **default flags** the node alone was enough in a production test (bf16 24.5 GB: about 30 s with the node, did not finish without it).\nTo force a small budget, start ComfyUI with (example: target ~4 GB resident):\n\n`--vram-headroom 20 --disable-pinned-memory`\n\n- `--vram-headroom N` = GB of VRAM ComfyUI must leave free. Weights are streamed layer by layer into the rest.\n  For a target budget of X GB use `N = (your total VRAM) - X`.\n- `--disable-pinned-memory` stops pinned host RAM from showing up as \"shared GPU memory\".\n\nCompare: run once **without** the flags and once with, and watch VRAM (nvidia-smi / Task Manager). Also try bypassing the node.\nMeasured here (24.5 GB bf16): ~21.5 GB over idle without flags; with flags **~1.6 GB at 1024x1024**. This graph is 2048x2048 (4 MP):\nstock ComfyUI (with the tiled VAE) ran under a hard 3 GB cap and failed at 2 GB; with the node it ran at 2 GB and failed at 1 GB,\nso the node buys about 1 GB at 4 MP. At 1024x1024 the flags alone are enough (stock, no node, ran down to a 1 GB cap).\nWith chunking the output is numerically equivalent but not bit-identical (bf16 matmul rounding; ~38-41 dB vs stock, visually\nthe same) and it is slower. Smaller chunks = less VRAM but slower. See the README in the node folder for all numbers and limits.\n\nSet the model names in the loader nodes to your own files first."
   ]
  }
